Dialogue: 1,0:00:00.37,0:00:02.25,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Graffitty is a terminal emulator,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:02.25,0:00:04.22,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}with a tiling manager and Chromium.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:07.12,0:00:08.72,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}But if you zoom out, you'll notice
Dialogue: 1,0:00:08.72,0:00:10.53,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}there's much more space to work with.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:11.60,0:00:13.98,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Each pane shows CPU, RAM usage,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:13.98,0:00:15.47,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and agent status.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:16.30,0:00:18.40,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Stash a pane outside the main view.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:19.52,0:00:21.72,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You can return to the main view at any time.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:22.77,0:00:24.47,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Or jump back to your previous view
Dialogue: 1,0:00:24.47,0:00:25.97,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and take a closer look at any pane.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:32.35,0:00:34.07,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}The builtin filetree is synchronised
Dialogue: 1,0:00:34.07,0:00:35.65,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}with the shell's current working directory.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:39.83,0:00:42.28,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You can edit the file name like editing a vim buffer,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:42.28,0:00:43.57,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and save to apply it.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:49.37,0:00:51.27,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Opening the files opens Neovim,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:51.27,0:00:54.20,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}or Chromium to preview all kinds of media.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:03.10,0:01:05.30,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You can also send file paths to the terminal,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:05.30,0:01:07.88,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}allowing you to change directory or open files.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:09.13,0:01:10.75,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Get creative with Chromium.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:10.75,0:01:12.62,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}It's a versatile viewer that handles things
Dialogue: 1,0:01:12.62,0:01:15.12,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}traditional terminals often need workarounds for.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:15.30,0:01:18.13,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}For example, use the MarkdownPreview plugin
Dialogue: 1,0:01:18.13,0:01:20.43,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}in Neovim to open Graffitty Chromium.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:20.43,0:01:22.37,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Now you have a live Markdown preview
Dialogue: 1,0:01:22.37,0:01:24.02,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}right inside your terminal.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:28.87,0:01:30.92,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Ask Claude to explain with a diagram.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:30.92,0:01:33.82,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Graffitty Chromium will open right beside your terminal.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:46.12,0:01:47.32,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Multiple agents can each use
Dialogue: 1,0:01:47.32,0:01:49.13,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}an isolated Chromium session,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:49.13,0:01:51.53,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}so they can run end-to-end tests in parallel.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:57.23,0:01:59.42,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You can also use Chromium's native interface
Dialogue: 1,0:01:59.42,0:02:00.72,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}in a separate window.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:00.88,0:02:02.77,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Closing that window doesn't close Chromium.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:02.77,0:02:05.42,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}It simply brings the same chromium back into Graffitty.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:08.90,0:02:11.83,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}One thing I find frustrating about agentic coding
Dialogue: 1,0:02:11.93,0:02:14.87,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}is how quickly agents can open a jumble of windows.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:15.15,0:02:17.10,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}They get in the way, steal focus,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:17.10,0:02:20.55,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and make it hard to tell which agent is using which browser.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:20.68,0:02:22.72,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}That's why Graffitty shows clear ownership
Dialogue: 1,0:02:22.72,0:02:24.18,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}with a connecting line.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:24.18,0:02:26.13,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You can move chromiums out of the main view
Dialogue: 1,0:02:26.15,0:02:27.75,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and still see who's using them.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:30.92,0:02:33.58,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}The down arrow gives you a summary of every tab,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:33.58,0:02:35.92,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}including its panes, CPU and RAM usage,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:35.92,0:02:37.12,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and agent status.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:37.12,0:02:40.52,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Type to fuzzy search tab and pane titles.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:42.38,0:02:45.03,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Aren't you already used to Vim's command line?
Dialogue: 1,0:02:45.03,0:02:46.20,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}The same thing is here,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:46.20,0:02:47.98,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}with a fuzzy finder built in.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:47.98,0:02:49.08,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You no longer have to keep
Dialogue: 1,0:02:49.08,0:02:51.32,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}every terminal feature as a key map.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:51.32,0:02:53.67,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Search commands, switch tabs, or jump to a pane.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:54.87,0:02:56.72,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Just like tmux keeps your session alive
Dialogue: 1,0:02:56.72,0:02:58.15,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}on an SSH server,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:58.15,0:02:59.53,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}you can detach from Graffitty
Dialogue: 1,0:02:59.53,0:03:01.15,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and attach again any time.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:01.15,0:03:02.73,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}It even has mobile clients,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:02.73,0:03:04.25,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}so you can code wherever you are.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:08.95,0:03:10.87,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Let me explain how the graph feature works.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:10.87,0:03:13.68,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Each terminal pane can advertise itself as a node,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:13.68,0:03:15.87,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}so it can queue messages to another pane.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:16.17,0:03:19.28,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}For example, Codex on the left implements a feature
Dialogue: 1,0:03:19.28,0:03:21.43,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and sends its work to Claude in the middle.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:22.03,0:03:24.00,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Claude only sends to the Slack node
Dialogue: 1,0:03:24.00,0:03:25.00,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}when a condition is met
Dialogue: 1,0:03:25.00,0:03:26.15,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}that the user needs to know about.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:33.47,0:03:36.33,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Now ask Codex to implement the website.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:38.08,0:03:39.17,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}When Codex finishes,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:39.32,0:03:41.88,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Claude receives its session and checks the work.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:48.62,0:03:50.17,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Claude finds missing requirements
Dialogue: 1,0:03:50.17,0:03:51.42,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and sends the review to Slack.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:54.82,0:03:56.43,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Graph is very useful when you want
Dialogue: 1,0:03:56.43,0:03:58.17,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}to quickly prototype a pipeline.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:58.32,0:04:00.12,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}This SystemOne node that calls
Dialogue: 1,0:04:00.12,0:04:01.43,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}the JEV decision model in the middle
Dialogue: 1,0:04:01.43,0:04:03.00,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}will route the session for you
Dialogue: 1,0:04:03.00,0:04:04.15,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}based on your question!
Dialogue: 1,0:04:15.23,0:04:16.43,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}The parallel node waits
Dialogue: 1,0:04:16.43,0:04:18.07,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}until every agent finishes.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:23.27,0:04:24.47,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Once all three finish,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:24.47,0:04:26.75,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}it sends their results and session references
Dialogue: 1,0:04:26.75,0:04:27.90,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}together to the reviewer.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:31.70,0:04:33.42,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}You can even make your own custom node
Dialogue: 1,0:04:33.42,0:04:36.02,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}very easily with Graffitty Graph Protocol.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:36.07,0:04:38.97,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}First, let's use the Payload Inspector node,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:38.97,0:04:41.01,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}which shows what data are sent through the graph.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:41.82,0:04:43.72,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}It sends just enough information
Dialogue: 1,0:04:43.72,0:04:45.15,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}for the next node to process,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:45.15,0:04:47.93,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}like the session file path and the last message.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:49.13,0:04:51.02,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Here is a minimal Python example
Dialogue: 1,0:04:51.02,0:04:52.68,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}that uses the GGP protocol
Dialogue: 1,0:04:52.68,0:04:55.22,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}to convert the data to uppercase.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:56.33,0:04:58.30,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Define the input and output ports,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:58.45,0:05:00.48,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}process the data, and send it on.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:01.73,0:05:02.15,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Run it.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:02.67,0:05:03.96,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Connect the nodes,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:05.72,0:05:06.63,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Send the message.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:11.88,0:05:14.68,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}This node converts the message to uppercase.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:14.68,0:05:17.00,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}The Payload Inspector shows the result.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:17.08,0:05:17.84,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}That's it.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:21.05,0:05:22.95,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}This makes your graph pipeline versatile.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:23.07,0:05:24.77,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}For example, create a simple watcher node
Dialogue: 1,0:05:24.77,0:05:27.45,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}that triggers your agent when a condition is met.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:33.97,0:05:35.44,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}Connect the watcher to Claude.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:36.27,0:05:37.67,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}A new email arrives,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:37.67,0:05:40.37,Voice,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,1008)}and Claude makes a podcast just as it's told to!
